One prediction per boosting round
iterationrange = c(1, step + 1) tells xgboost to predict with only the first step trees. The gist used the older ntreelimit = step - 1, but ntreelimit = 0 means “all trees”, so its first frame showed the final fit. Here step 0 is the starting point, the base_score, and steps past the last tree (the untuned model has 15) keep the full model.

dados_xgb &lt;- dados %&gt;% select(x) %&gt;% as.matrix()

predict_xgb &lt;- function(ajuste, step) {
  pred &lt;- if (step == 0) {
    rep(mean(dados$y), nrow(dados))
  } else {
    xgboost:::predict.xgb.Booster(ajuste$fit, newdata = dados_xgb, iterationrange = c(1, min(step, ajuste$fit$niter) + 1))
  }
  dados %&gt;% mutate(pred = pred)
}

df &lt;- expand_grid(modelo = names(ajustes), step = 0:trees) %&gt;%
  mutate(preds = map2(modelo, step, ~ predict_xgb(ajustes[[.x]], .y))) %&gt;%
  unnest(preds) %&gt;%
  mutate(modelo = factor(modelo, levels = names(ajustes)))

Findings

Countries like Canada and Japan seem to be improving;
Brazil has little data, but doesn’t stand out positively;
Awards and IMDB ratings are no different between movies that passed the test and movies that didn’t. One could argue that gender discrimination, while always unjustifiable, can’t even rely on the rhetoric that it pays off financially;
